No one would sell him an airplane or an engine,

788
00:35:43.670 --> 00:35:46.260
'cause they were so scared that he would fail

789
00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:48.060
and give them a bad reputation.

790
00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:50.907
He makes the flight 33 and a half hours

791
00:35:50.907 --> 00:35:55.263
from a Roosevelt field to Le Bourget.

792
00:35:55.263 --> 00:35:57.730
Becomes the most famous humans on the planet,

793
00:35:57.730 --> 00:35:59.960
aviation skyrockets.

794
00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:01.350
And it was cause of that prize.

795
00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:02.801
And I was like, that's what I'm gonna do

796
00:36:02.801 --> 00:36:03.660
I'm gonna create a prize.

797
00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:08.440
And so I announced the idea of a $10 million prize

798
00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:11.990
for the first team who could build a private spaceship,

799
00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:14.500
carry three adults up to a hundred kilometers,

800
00:36:14.500 --> 00:36:17.380
land and do it again within two weeks.

801
00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:21.233
And lo and behold, we had 26 teams from seven countries,

802
00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:23.410
guy named Burt Rutan,

803
00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:24.730
who had built the Voyager airplane

804
00:36:24.730 --> 00:36:26.110
that flew nonstop around the world,

805
00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:29.870
backed by Microsoft co-founder, Paul Allen,

806
00:36:29.870 --> 00:36:34.500
built SpaceShipOne and claimed the prize in 2004.

807
00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:38.140
I think the legacy of that was one,

808
00:36:38.140 --> 00:36:42.230
we had to change all the regulations

809
00:36:42.230 --> 00:36:43.749
and that was a big deal.

810
00:36:43.749 --> 00:36:47.040
The regulations for commercial private human space life

811
00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:48.430
did not exist.

812
00:36:48.430 --> 00:36:50.840
And we worked, we said, listen,

813
00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:54.040
this price is gonna be won in Russia or Argentina.

814
00:36:54.040 --> 00:36:55.400
So we got the laws changed here

815
00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:59.727
and then most other countries copied as they do.

816
00:36:59.727 --> 00:37:01.900
The FAA is usually the leader here.

817
00:37:01.900 --> 00:37:03.671
Was that part of why it took that many years

818
00:37:03.671 --> 00:37:05.700
or was it just the technological development?

819
00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:06.681
No, it's the technology,

820
00:37:06.681 --> 00:37:09.980
it was the capital that was required,

821
00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:13.410
aggregating the capital to build these ships was,

822
00:37:13.410 --> 00:37:14.498
'cause everybody said, listen,

823
00:37:14.498 --> 00:37:16.610
can anyone really pull it off?

824
00:37:16.610 --> 00:37:18.070
Why isn't NASA doing it?

825
00:37:18.070 --> 00:37:19.950
And aren't you gonna die trying?

826
00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:23.290
And so that just made it very risk averse for everybody.

827
00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:26.360
And as a result of that,

828
00:37:26.360 --> 00:37:29.440
it took eight years for the prize to get one.

829
00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:30.979
Branson came in, bought the rights

830
00:37:30.979 --> 00:37:34.020
to create Virgin Galactic.

831
00:37:34.020 --> 00:37:36.870
And then I've known Jeff Bezos since college.

832
00:37:36.870 --> 00:37:39.200
My first group ever was College Space Organization.

833
00:37:39.200 --> 00:37:41.700
I was one of the MIT chapters, one of the present chapter.

834
00:37:41.700 --> 00:37:43.210
'Cause you had this double life

835
00:37:43.210 --> 00:37:46.018
through College and med school,

836
00:37:46.018 --> 00:37:49.580
in the space world, starting all these organizations.

837
00:37:49.580 --> 00:37:50.413
And then going through medical school

838
00:37:50.413 --> 00:37:52.600
and trying to make my parents happy.

839
00:37:52.600 --> 00:37:55.070
I remember I met with Bezos in Seattle

840
00:37:55.070 --> 00:37:57.250
after like the year after he started Amazon

841
00:37:57.250 --> 00:37:58.217
and I was like, "So Jeff,

842
00:37:58.217 --> 00:37:59.227
"what up with Amazon?

843
00:37:59.227 --> 00:38:00.440
"I thought you wanted to do space?"

844
00:38:00.440 --> 00:38:02.847
And he goes, "Well, I'm gonna make my money in Amazon,

845
00:38:02.847 --> 00:38:04.000
"then I'll go spend it in space."

846
00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:05.780
Like a simple, two step plan.

847
00:38:05.780 --> 00:38:07.970
Right, that clarity all the way back then.

848
00:38:07.970 --> 00:38:11.340
Yeah and then he and Elon

849
00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:15.283
who I've known through since 2000,

850
00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:20.230
I was trying to get Elon to fund the Amir Ansari XPRISE

851
00:38:20.230 --> 00:38:23.870
before I had the money and then the Ansari family funded it.

852
00:38:23.870 --> 00:38:27.200
But this idea that you can inspire small teams

853
00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:30.730
to do crazy things is huge.

854
00:38:30.730 --> 00:38:34.010
And bringing this back to health and longevity,

855
00:38:34.010 --> 00:38:36.101
working with David Sinclair and George Church,

856
00:38:36.101 --> 00:38:38.877
we've designed and developed

857
00:38:38.877 --> 00:38:42.963
and initially funded by Sergey Young to give him credit.

858
00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:45.353
We've got an age reversal XPRISE

859
00:38:45.353 --> 00:38:48.450
that we're very close to launching.

860
00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:50.660
And when Sergey first went to do a longevity prize

861
00:38:50.660 --> 00:38:53.197
and I've been the biggest longevity fan

862
00:38:53.197 --> 00:38:56.660
involved in this industry for a decade now,

863
00:38:56.660 --> 00:38:58.083
I was like, man,

864
00:38:58.083 --> 00:39:01.200
I just don't see how we're gonna make an XPRISE out of it.

865
00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:03.380
And the more I started studying it

866
00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:08.030
and kudos to Sergey, I was like, oh, interesting.

867
00:39:08.030 --> 00:39:10.440
And it was okay, we're not gonna do a longevity prize.

868
00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:13.060
We're gonna do an age reversal prize

869
00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:14.460
and it's can we...

870
00:39:14.460 --> 00:39:15.440
And to win this prize,

871
00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:17.480
you need to demonstrably

872
00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:19.350
through a number of very specific mechanisms,

873
00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:24.170
demonstrate the age reversal of three independent tissues

874
00:39:24.170 --> 00:39:27.818
or organ systems in the human by 20 years or more

875
00:39:27.818 --> 00:39:29.930
in a repeatable fashion.

876
00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:32.190
Yeah, it's a pretty high bar,

877
00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:33.570
especially when all of the,

878
00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:35.250
it seems like all of the science right now

879
00:39:35.250 --> 00:39:38.450
is in mice and rats and the like.

880
00:39:38.450 --> 00:39:39.353
Yeah, it is.

881
00:39:40.993 --> 00:39:45.993
That work is going from mice and rats into dogs.

882
00:39:46.090 --> 00:39:49.780
And when I asked David and George

883
00:39:49.780 --> 00:39:54.060
again, my two sort of superstars

884
00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:56.310
and in the book "Life Force,"

885
00:39:56.310 --> 00:39:59.900
we have these heroes and David and George

886
00:39:59.900 --> 00:40:02.570
are definitive heroes in the book.

887
00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:05.980
Ask them, when do you think we're gonna be seeing

888
00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:08.920
the gene therapies

889
00:40:08.920 --> 00:40:12.293
that were used to reverse the age in mice?

890
00:40:14.600 --> 00:40:15.717
The answer is,

891
00:40:15.717 --> 00:40:18.797
"We'll likely see it being tested in humans

892
00:40:18.797 --> 00:40:20.270
"in five years time."

893
00:40:20.270 --> 00:40:21.520
Which sounds insane.

894
00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:25.820
Is that once again to kind of regulatory hurdles

895
00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:28.810
or more about the progress of the science itself?

896
00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:33.810
I think it's both, the FDA is a governor

897
00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:37.870
like limiting the speed by virtue

898
00:40:37.870 --> 00:40:41.560
of wanting to make sure it's done well

899
00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:44.800
and doing it safely.

900
00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:48.847
When I was in medical school and graduate school,

901
00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:54.210
I was in a Richard Mulligan's Lab

902
00:40:54.210 --> 00:40:57.320
at the Whitehead Institute at MIT in Harvard.

903
00:40:57.320 --> 00:41:00.070
And it was the earliest days of gene therapy

904
00:41:00.070 --> 00:41:04.210
and this incredibly brilliant man envision

905
00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:09.080
of being able to use a virus in this case,

906
00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:11.790
it had no associated virus,

907
00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:14.340
that you've stripped out the virus's DNA

908
00:41:15.210 --> 00:41:17.290
and put in the DNA that you want,

909
00:41:17.290 --> 00:41:21.307
and using this virus to go and infect the specific cells

910
00:41:21.307 --> 00:41:22.600
in the human body

911
00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:25.140
and inject this piece of programming into it,

912
00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:27.353
was a massively brilliant idea.

913
00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:32.123
And unfortunately, when it was first tested,

914
00:41:33.030 --> 00:41:34.913
it killed the young patient.

915
00:41:36.100 --> 00:41:38.240
And it really set back the field

916
00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:43.110
like five to 10 years,

917
00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:45.676
I don't know the exact number.

918
00:41:45.676 --> 00:41:50.060
And then it was eventually successfully trialed

919
00:41:50.060 --> 00:41:51.830
in what's called bubble boy's disease

920
00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:54.213
when you have no immune system.

921
00:41:54.213 --> 00:41:55.160
And it was successful.

922
00:41:55.160 --> 00:41:59.543
And now it's an extraordinary tool in the toolkit.

923
00:41:59.543 --> 00:42:02.060
But you don't wanna make that mistake.

924
00:42:02.060 --> 00:42:04.940
You don't want to have something set back the field.

925
00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:07.560
So there's caution in doing it

926
00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:12.560
in mice and rats and Guinea pigs and dogs and primates,

927
00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:14.550
and then eventually humans.

928
00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:16.780
Well, there's certainly a qualitative difference

929
00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:21.780
between extending the life of cells, human cells

930
00:42:24.438 --> 00:42:29.100
or decreasing the rate at which they decline.

931
00:42:29.100 --> 00:42:30.640
And it's another thing altogether

932
00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:34.010
to "reverse their aging."

933
00:42:34.010 --> 00:42:37.390
So how do you demarcate the difference between those two?

934
00:42:37.390 --> 00:42:38.943
So here's the question.

935
00:42:38.943 --> 00:42:43.943
And it's one that when I first thought about it

936
00:42:44.130 --> 00:42:45.610
and I'm not sure if I heard it posed

937
00:42:45.610 --> 00:42:47.750
or just my mind came up with it,

938
00:42:47.750 --> 00:42:50.853
which is when we're born,

939
00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:54.540
we have 3.2 billion letters from our mother,

940
00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:56.490
and 3.2 from our father,

941
00:42:56.490 --> 00:43:00.743
and that same genome is there when we're 20 and 40

942
00:43:00.743 --> 00:43:03.300
and 60 and 80 and a hundred.

943
00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:05.760
And the question is if it's the same genome

944
00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:08.096
and it is basically the same genome,

945
00:43:08.096 --> 00:43:10.290
why do you look different?

946
00:43:10.290 --> 00:43:11.423
What, why?

947
00:43:12.325 --> 00:43:15.550
Then it turns out it's not your genome,

948
00:43:15.550 --> 00:43:17.310
it's your epigenome.

949
00:43:17.310 --> 00:43:19.560
Epi from the Greek word for above,

950
00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:23.047
it's the controls of which genes are turned on

951
00:43:23.047 --> 00:43:24.550
and which genes are turned off.

952
00:43:24.550 --> 00:43:26.570
So as you well know,

953
00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:30.980
every cell in our body has the genes

954
00:43:30.980 --> 00:43:34.387
to become any other kind of cell in our body.

955
00:43:34.387 --> 00:43:36.820
And when we begin life,

956
00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:39.805
we are a pluripotent stem cell,

957
00:43:39.805 --> 00:43:42.280
stemness means the ability to differentiate

958
00:43:42.280 --> 00:43:46.943
into bone, muscle, ligament, whatever it might be.

959
00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:51.870
But once it's differentiated,

960
00:43:51.870 --> 00:43:56.870
that cell is only expressing the proteins for a muscle cell

961
00:43:58.130 --> 00:44:02.690
or only expressing the proteins for a skin cell.

962
00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:05.260
The other proteins that are used in the eye

963
00:44:05.260 --> 00:44:08.445
or the brain or the liver are wrapped up

964
00:44:08.445 --> 00:44:12.780
and tightly bound in this system

965
00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:17.260
called our histones that limit it from being expressed.

966
00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:19.930
And so it's why your cells

967
00:44:19.930 --> 00:44:24.410
don't all of a sudden start becoming other types of cells.

968
00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:25.390
You don't want,

969
00:44:25.390 --> 00:44:28.464
sort of your muscle becoming skin cells

970
00:44:28.464 --> 00:44:32.330
and that's your epigenome.

971
00:44:32.330 --> 00:44:35.580
And what David Sinclair does a beautiful job

972
00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:38.920
in his book "Lifespan," which I command to everybody

973
00:44:40.770 --> 00:44:45.613
in discussing that our epigenome changes over time.

974
00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:50.470
And you can measure your epigenome,

975
00:44:50.470 --> 00:44:52.470
looking at methylation patterns,

976
00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:55.110
the methyl groups of CH3

977
00:44:55.110 --> 00:44:57.170
attached to different parts of your DNA

978
00:44:57.170 --> 00:44:58.570
that control whether it can be red or not red,

979
00:44:58.570 --> 00:45:01.451
and whether it's wrapped and tightly or not.

980
00:45:01.451 --> 00:45:03.640
And he then goes on and talk about

981
00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:07.833
what a system called a Sirtuin systems, right?

982
00:45:07.833 --> 00:45:12.110
There are seven Sirtuin genes and seven Sirtuin proteins

983
00:45:13.820 --> 00:45:18.820
that are controlling two different functions

984
00:45:19.120 --> 00:45:21.360
that are critical to your existence.

985
00:45:21.360 --> 00:45:25.140
One function that the sirtuins control is your epigenome.

986
00:45:25.140 --> 00:45:26.980
The sirtuins are controlling,

987
00:45:26.980 --> 00:45:29.230
keeping your muscle cell a muscle cell,

988
00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:31.950
keeping your skin cell a skin cell.

989
00:45:31.950 --> 00:45:36.782
The other thing that sirtuins are controlling is DNA repair.

990
00:45:36.782 --> 00:45:41.782
So just living life, being hit by cosmic rays,

991
00:45:43.470 --> 00:45:46.320
by chemicals in the environment, by secondhand smoke,

992
00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:47.720
whatever it might be,

993
00:45:47.720 --> 00:45:50.380
we're constantly being hit by these mutagens

994
00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:52.340
that are causing double strand breaks

995
00:45:52.340 --> 00:45:53.440
and single strand breaks

996
00:45:53.440 --> 00:45:55.570
and other kinds of DNA damage.

997
00:45:55.570 --> 00:45:56.800
The number I just read the other day

998
00:45:56.800 --> 00:46:01.800
is a thousand to a million DNA hits per day per cell.

999
00:46:03.323 --> 00:46:04.870
I mean, it's insane.

1000
00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:07.240
But luckily our cells have evolved

1001
00:46:07.240 --> 00:46:08.933
these DNA repair mechanisms.

1002
00:46:09.990 --> 00:46:13.490
And our Sirtuins are over seeing that

1003
00:46:13.490 --> 00:46:15.859
and supporting a DNA repair.

1004
00:46:15.859 --> 00:46:19.920
And as we get older,

1005
00:46:19.920 --> 00:46:24.390
the DNA repair is getting more and more burdensome

1006
00:46:24.390 --> 00:46:27.760
and it's distracting the sirtuins from their other function

1007
00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:30.093
of epigenetic regulation.

1008
00:46:31.220 --> 00:46:32.620
But it's even worse than that

1009
00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:35.970
because the fuel that sirtuins are using

1010
00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:37.217
is something called NAD.

1011
00:46:38.310 --> 00:46:40.540
And we can talk about NMN which is a precursor.

1012
00:46:40.540 --> 00:46:45.190
And as you age your NAD levels in your cells plummet

1013
00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:47.140
to under 50%,

1014
00:46:47.140 --> 00:46:49.820
again, in our forties, fifties, and sixties,

1015
00:46:49.820 --> 00:46:52.170
because our body was never designed to live that long.

1016
00:46:52.170 --> 00:46:54.240
There was no homoestasis at that point.

1017
00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:58.130
And so the way I visualize it in my mind

1018
00:46:58.130 --> 00:47:00.453
is your genome is like the keys of a piano.

1019
00:47:01.830 --> 00:47:03.773
Your epigenome is the piano player,

1020
00:47:04.750 --> 00:47:07.647
and if you're playing the right keys at the right time,

1021
00:47:07.647 --> 00:47:10.990
you're expressing yourself properly.

1022
00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:13.660
Now imagine that the piano player at the same time,

1023
00:47:13.660 --> 00:47:15.290
they're playing the concerto

1024
00:47:15.290 --> 00:47:18.410
need to go and repair this thing over here.

1025
00:47:18.410 --> 00:47:20.548
And the amount of time they're having to repair

1026
00:47:20.548 --> 00:47:23.960
is they're being distracted over and over

1027
00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:25.877
and more and more frequently.

1028
00:47:25.877 --> 00:47:29.860
And then imagine that the food that they're being fed

1029
00:47:29.860 --> 00:47:31.680
to energize them is getting less and less.

1030
00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:32.960
They're becoming weaker and weaker

1031
00:47:32.960 --> 00:47:34.218
and more and more distracted.

1032
00:47:34.218 --> 00:47:37.720
And so your epigenome gets dysregulated

1033
00:47:37.720 --> 00:47:42.390
and we then see skin changes, we see more cancer,

1034
00:47:42.390 --> 00:47:47.390
we see a whole slew of different age-related situations.

1035
00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:50.632
So the idea being that by ingesting

1036
00:47:50.632 --> 00:47:55.632
this exogenous NAD precursor in the form of NMN,

1037
00:47:56.150 --> 00:47:58.230
you're alleviating some of that burden

1038
00:47:58.230 --> 00:48:00.510
and allowing a better regulation.

1039
00:48:00.510 --> 00:48:02.753
You're giving your sirtuins more energy

1040
00:48:02.753 --> 00:48:06.423
to fight their good fight.

1041
00:48:06.423 --> 00:48:09.330
And what is the relationship between all of that

1042
00:48:09.330 --> 00:48:14.330
and this conversation around declining telomere lengths

1043
00:48:14.550 --> 00:48:17.930
and how that impacts our aging?

1044
00:48:17.930 --> 00:48:21.420
So I won't dive into areas

1045
00:48:21.420 --> 00:48:23.350
that I'm not fully sure about.

1046
00:48:23.350 --> 00:48:27.960
But I mean, telomeres are the end caps of our cells

1047
00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:31.460
that get shorter and shorter as we age.

1048
00:48:31.460 --> 00:48:34.200
So there's something called the hay flick limit,

1049
00:48:34.200 --> 00:48:37.643
which is your cells are able to replicate about 50 times.

